  GREEN  GKPINSPANPOS     BLOCKING: a constructed predicate with both anchor literals INLINED is accepted by his in-span filter: sel=true mov=true both=true. The filter can fire
  GREEN  GKPINSPANNEG     the matched negative — same predicate, the movable anchor replaced by a pinned rev — is NOT in-span for movable (sel=true mov=false), so the arm is not firing on everything
  GREEN  GKPINSPAN        his own blocking KP, the one his §3 exists to make the zero mean something, reads both-in-span=false (sel false · mov false) when its predicate line is put through the in-span filter — while the SAME text through the candidate arm reads sel true · mov true. The filter that publishes "0 of 11" has no known positive
  GREEN  GDIFFFILTER      his wide selector arm matches an ADDING-commit read as if it were a recency selector: `--diff-filter=A ... .pop()` on a newest-first list is the OLDEST entry and is stable, yet SELECTOR_WIDE arms [3] match it (the real recency line matches arms [0,3]). So GSTALEPIN is a FALSE POSITIVE of the selector arm, not an artifact of the expansion's reach
  GREEN  GINSPANBASE      tree-wide, over 670 leg sites and not only the 11 candidates, the in-span conjunction holds 0 times (0.0%): selector-in-span 3, movable-in-span 11, either 14. A filter whose positive rate over the whole tree is 0.0% is measuring the tree's hoisted-operand coding style, not the ordering
  GREEN  GRESOLVEONLY     every one of the 11 candidates reaches at least one of its two anchors ONLY after identifier resolution — 11 of 11. The candidate arm resolves bindings; the in-span filter does not, so no candidate in a hoisted-operand tree can pass it
  GREEN  GWHYARM          1 of 11 candidates carry an anchor that is in the why-string arm and NOT in the boolean operand: [GTUPLE sel]. A why-string is not a conjunct. His `prose-arg-call` flag caught one shape of this; the arm split measures it per candidate
